WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:03.110
On the outside, it may seem like

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:06.252
we haven't changed much
from our early ancestors.

00:00:06.252 --> 00:00:10.770
But take a closer look and
you'll discover that humans

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:14.770
are still evolving in
some fascinating new ways.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.203
The average person can hold
their breath for 30 seconds.

00:00:22.203 --> 00:00:24.750
But Bajau people in Southeast Asia

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.960
can hold it up to 12 minutes

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.210
which comes in handy when they free dive

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.450
over 230 feet for food.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.350
Part of the reason is simply
training and experience

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.193
since they spend over half
their workday underwater.

00:00:38.193 --> 00:00:42.260
But it turns out, the Bajau
also have a genetic mutation

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.110
that makes their spleens
50% larger than average.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.520
Our spleens are what filter

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.336
and store oxygen-rich red blood cells.

00:00:50.336 --> 00:00:53.131
So, when the Bajau dive underwater,

00:00:53.131 --> 00:00:56.860
their larger spleens contract,
which boosts oxygen levels

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.200
in the blood by up to 10%,

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.730
allowing them more time to collect food.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.110
It's a handy adaptation that probably came

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.360
from living on houseboats
for the last 1,000 years.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:14.740
About 35% of the world's
population has the equivalent

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.720
of a genetic superpower.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.773
They can digest milk as adults.

00:01:19.773 --> 00:01:21.098
Not impressed?

00:01:21.098 --> 00:01:22.868
Then consider this.

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:25.230
Humans are among the only mammals

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.615
on the planet with this ability.

00:01:27.623 --> 00:01:30.630
Thanks to a genetic mutation
that popped up in Europe

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.244
around 5,000 - 10,000 years ago.

00:01:33.244 --> 00:01:36.700
Those with the mutation produce lactase

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.812
throughout their lives.

00:01:38.812 --> 00:01:41.100
That's the enzyme that
breaks down milk sugars

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.070
for easy digestion.

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:45.320
And for most of history,

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.640
humans stopped producing
lactase after childhood.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.590
But once those lucky few
Europeans could drink milk

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.150
as adults, that suddenly
meant extra nutrition

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:57.920
and ultimately healthier, longer lives

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.830
so that they could pass their superpower

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.960
on to future generations.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:06.960
Today, roughly 95% of northern
Europeans have the mutation.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.202
The human body has an estimated
20,000 - 30,000 genes.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.688
And while some mutations can
change our spleens and diet,

00:02:18.688 --> 00:02:23.573
others can protect us against
deadly conditions, like AIDS.

00:02:23.573 --> 00:02:27.526
Nearly 50% of women in parts
of South Africa have HIV.

00:02:27.526 --> 00:02:30.260
But researchers found that
some show stronger resistance

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.300
to the virus,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.010
taking longer to develop AIDS

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.848
or sometimes never getting it at all.

00:02:35.856 --> 00:02:39.610
Turns out, these women also
share a mutation in the gene

00:02:39.610 --> 00:02:44.544
called HLA-B27 that the others lack.

00:02:44.544 --> 00:02:48.447
Are we going to evolve
our way to HIV immunity?

00:02:48.447 --> 00:02:50.308
It's too early to say.

00:02:50.308 --> 00:02:52.413
But one thing's clear.

00:02:52.413 --> 00:02:54.636
Evolution's not done with us yet.

